      <p>A good <strong>tiny house layout</strong> does three things: it keeps one open zone for daily living, it groups all the plumbing on one wall, and it hides storage inside things you already need (stairs, benches, beds). Get those right and a 200 sq ft home on wheels or a 700 sq ft cottage will feel larger than its footprint. The tiny house layout ideas below are the moves designers use most, from galley kitchens and storage stairs to flex rooms and gooseneck bedrooms, with notes on when each one works and when it does not.</p>

      <div class="key-takeaways">
        <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Stack the kitchen, bath and laundry on one wet wall. It is the single biggest saver of cost and space in tiny house floor plans.</li>
          <li>Decide early between a loft and a main-floor bedroom. It drives the whole layout, the roof height and your stair design.</li>
          <li>Keep one clear sightline from the entry to a window at the far end; it makes a small room read as big.</li>
          <li>Every fixed object should store something: stairs, benches, the bed platform, even the walls.</li>
          <li>Test a layout at full scale before you buy plans. Our free <a href="/tools/tiny-house-layout-planner/">layout planner</a> lets you drag rooms and furniture onto a scaled grid.</li>
