      <h2 id="principles">Tiny house layout principles before you start</h2>
      <p>Most tiny house floor plans fall into one of two families. <strong>Tiny houses on wheels</strong> are limited to about 8.5 ft wide, so they are long, narrow and arranged in a line from end to end. <strong>Tiny homes on foundations</strong>, such as <a href="/tiny-cottage-plans/">cottages</a>, <a href="/small-cabin-plans/">cabins</a> and <a href="/adu-plans/">ADUs</a>, can be 16&ndash;24 ft wide, which opens up side-by-side rooms and a proper hallway. The ideas below note which family each one suits.</p>
      <p>Three rules apply to both. Keep the main circulation path at least 30&ndash;36 in wide. Put the biggest window on the wall you see when you walk in. And plan furniture and appliances first, then walls, because a sofa, fridge and bed are the dimensions you cannot shrink.</p>

      <h2 id="kitchen-ideas">Tiny house kitchen layout ideas</h2>

      <h3 id="idea-1">1. The galley kitchen along both long walls</h3>
      <p>On an 8.5 ft wide trailer, two parallel counters with an aisle between them is the most efficient kitchen there is. Put the sink and range on one side and the fridge, pantry and prep space on the other. Aim for an aisle of around 36&ndash;42 in: narrower feels tight with two cooks, wider wastes counter. A galley also makes a natural divider between the living room and the bathroom at the far end.</p>

