      <h3 id="idea-2">2. The L-shaped kitchen at the end</h3>
      <p>Put the kitchen across one end of the house and wrap the counter around the corner. You get one long run of uninterrupted counter, the end-wall window over the sink, and the middle of the house stays open for living and dining. This works best in homes 24 ft and longer, and in wider foundation homes where an L can grow into a U with a small peninsula that doubles as a breakfast bar.</p>

      <h2 id="sleeping-ideas">Bedroom and loft layout ideas</h2>

      <h3 id="idea-3">3. Main-floor bedroom vs sleeping loft</h3>
      <p>This is the biggest decision in any small floor plan. A <strong>loft</strong> frees the whole main floor for living but means climbing a ladder or stairs every night, sitting-height headroom and summer heat. A <strong>main-floor bedroom</strong> costs 50&ndash;80 sq ft of floor space but suits anyone with bad knees, aging parents or a preference for standing up to get dressed. In a tiny house on wheels, a main-floor bedroom usually means a 28 ft or longer trailer. On a foundation home, single-level plans like <a href="/plans/plan-12-2417/">Plan 12-2417</a> put everything on one floor with no stairs at all. See <a href="/tiny-house-plans-with-loft/">tiny house plans with a loft</a> for headroom and egress rules if you go up.</p>

