      <h3 id="idea-4">4. Double lofts at each end</h3>
      <p>Put a loft over each end of a tiny house on wheels and leave the middle open to the roof. One loft is the main bedroom, the other is a kids&rsquo; room, guest bed or storage. The tall center gives the living area a cathedral feel that offsets the low ceilings under the lofts. Keep the bathroom and kitchen under the lofts, since those spaces do not need a high ceiling.</p>

      <h3 id="idea-5">5. A Murphy bed in the living room</h3>
      <p>A wall bed that folds up into a cabinet turns the living room into a bedroom at night, with no loft to climb. It works well for one or two people who are disciplined about making the bed each morning. Plan a clear rectangle of floor in front of it that is about the size of the mattress plus 18 in, and do not put a rug or coffee table there that you will have to move every night.</p>

      <h3 id="idea-6">6. The gooseneck bedroom</h3>
      <p>A gooseneck trailer has a raised deck that extends over the bed of the tow truck. On a tiny house, that raised section becomes a bedroom a few steps up from the main floor, with more headroom than a loft and no ladder. The trade-off is a heavier trailer, a truck with a gooseneck hitch, and a longer overall length. Our <a href="/blog/tiny-house-trailer-size-guide/">tiny house trailer size guide</a> covers gooseneck lengths and weights.</p>

      <h2 id="storage-ideas">Storage and stair layout ideas</h2>

      <h3 id="idea-7">7. Storage stairs instead of a ladder</h3>
      <p>Stairs take more floor space than a ladder, but each tread can hold a drawer or cubby, and the space under the top landing can house a closet, washer or fridge. Done well, storage stairs replace a closet and a dresser. Keep risers consistent (around 9&ndash;10 in is common in tiny homes) and add a handrail. Alternating-tread stairs and ship&rsquo;s ladders save floor space but are harder to climb with an armful of laundry.</p>

      <h3 id="idea-8">8. Built-in benches with lift-up seats</h3>
      <p>Benches along a wall or under a window seat more people than chairs in less space, and the cavity underneath stores bedding, tools or a water heater. A built-in banquette with a fold-down table is one of the best per-square-foot investments in tiny house floor plans.</p>

      <h3 id="idea-9">9. Go vertical with storage</h3>
      <p>Floor space is scarce; wall space up to the ceiling is not. Run shelves and cabinets to the ceiling, put a rail of hooks above the kitchen counter, and use the space above doors and windows. A high shelf around the perimeter of the living room holds books and rarely used items without crowding the floor. Keep heavy items low and in closed cabinets in a home that travels.</p>

      <h2 id="room-ideas">Bathroom, room and flexible-space layout ideas</h2>

      <h3 id="idea-10">10. A flex room with a retractable wall</h3>
      <p>On a foundation home, a small room next to the great room can serve as a dining room, home office or guest bedroom, depending on whether a sliding or folding wall is open or closed. Both <a href="/plans/plan-12-2418/">Plan 12-2418</a> and <a href="/plans/plan-12-2417/">Plan 12-2417</a> use this idea: a flex room with a retractable wall off the open living area, so the home lives larger day to day and still has a private room when guests arrive.</p>

