      <h3 id="idea-11">11. Stack every wet fixture on one wall</h3>
      <p>Put the kitchen sink, shower, toilet, washer and water heater back-to-back along a single wall. Drain and supply runs stay short, there is one place to insulate against freezing, and you save money on fittings and labor. In the galley diagram above, the bathroom sits right behind the sink. In a two-story home, stack an upstairs bath directly over the downstairs one.</p>

      <h3 id="idea-12">12. Bathroom at the hitch end</h3>
      <p>On a bumper-pull trailer, roughly 10&ndash;15% of the loaded weight should sit on the tongue for stable towing. Placing the bathroom and other heavy items (water heater, washer and any fresh-water tank) toward the hitch end helps you hit that target, and it keeps the rear free for a big window and the living room, where the view is. Run the numbers in the <a href="/tools/tiny-house-trailer-weight-calculator/">trailer weight calculator</a> before you commit, since tongue weight should stay within the range your trailer and hitch are rated for.</p>

      <h3 id="idea-13">13. Pocket doors and barn doors instead of swing doors</h3>
      <p>A standard 30 in swinging door sweeps roughly 5 sq ft of floor you can never use for anything else. A pocket door slides into the wall and takes none. Barn doors are easier to retrofit but need a stretch of clear wall beside the opening and do not seal sound or smell as well, which matters on a bathroom.</p>

      <h3 id="idea-14">14. A convertible sofa and dining set</h3>
      <p>Furniture that changes job saves more space than any wall you can move: a sofa that becomes a guest bed, a coffee table that lifts to dining height, a wall-mounted drop-leaf table that folds flat. Pick one piece of convertible furniture per zone rather than filling the house with mechanisms you will stop using.</p>

      <h3 id="idea-15">15. The porch as an outdoor room</h3>
      <p>A covered porch is the cheapest square footage you can build: no insulation, plumbing or interior finish. Furnish it like a room, with seating, a light, an outlet and maybe screens, and the home gains a living area for most of the year. Both of our 20 x 38 ft plans include a covered porch, and <a href="/plans/plan-12-2418/">Plan 12-2418</a> can be flipped to put the porch at the rear. On a home on wheels, a fold-down deck does the same job at each stop.</p>

      <div class="callout wood">
        <strong>Rule of thumb</strong>
        <p>If you can only afford one trick, make it the wet wall. If you can afford two, add storage stairs or a flex room. Everything else is refinement.</p>
      </div>

